WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.333 --> 00:00:11.344
[Intro Music]

00:00:11.344 --> 00:00:13.847
NF: OK Toxic Avengers, accumulate!

00:00:13.847 --> 00:00:15.749
I'd like to call this
meeting to order.

00:00:15.749 --> 00:00:17.884
NF: First, I would like to
thank Admiral Murica for

00:00:17.884 --> 00:00:21.354
his tireless work to remove all
the asbestos from the cafeteria.

00:00:21.354 --> 00:00:22.555
NF: Job well done, soldier.

00:00:22.555 --> 00:00:24.190
NF: However, Bulk.

00:00:24.190 --> 00:00:26.192
NF: I hear you caused
substantial damage on

00:00:26.192 --> 00:00:27.627
our last field mission.

00:00:27.627 --> 00:00:29.763
Bulk: Bulk just trying
to save peoples from

00:00:29.763 --> 00:00:31.431
killer robot peoples.

00:00:31.431 --> 00:00:33.533
NF: [with outrage] That doesn't
explain the devouring of every

00:00:33.533 --> 00:00:36.202
bagel and donut
shop in Brooklyn!

00:00:36.202 --> 00:00:38.471
Bulk: [groaning] Bulk sorry.

00:00:38.471 --> 00:00:39.773
NF: [with authority] Go fix it!

00:00:39.773 --> 00:00:42.876
Bulk: Bulk just really
like donuts and bagels...

00:00:42.876 --> 00:00:44.878
[crowd laughter]

00:00:44.878 --> 00:00:46.513
Leadman: I have arrived.

00:00:46.513 --> 00:00:48.882
The meeting can start now.

00:00:48.882 --> 00:00:50.984
Black meadow: The meeting
started five minutes ago.

00:00:50.984 --> 00:00:52.085
As scheduled.

00:00:52.085 --> 00:00:53.153
Leadman: Hey!

00:00:53.153 --> 00:00:54.788
Relax Black Meadow.

00:00:54.788 --> 00:00:57.390
Let Leadman come
sit next to you.

00:00:57.390 --> 00:01:00.160
You'll feel better
about this in no time.

00:01:00.160 --> 00:01:02.295
Nick Fiery: Yeah,
about that Leadman.

00:01:02.295 --> 00:01:04.464
We're going to have to
do something about that.

00:01:04.464 --> 00:01:07.700
Lead is toxic to the humans
you are [-cough-] saving.

00:01:07.700 --> 00:01:08.401
Leadman: What?

00:01:08.401 --> 00:01:10.136
No way, eye patch!

00:01:10.136 --> 00:01:11.704
This is my lead suit.

00:01:11.704 --> 00:01:13.106
Leadman: The ladies dig it!

00:01:13.106 --> 00:01:14.274
Black meadow: [Gags].

00:01:14.274 --> 00:01:14.874
Leadman: Hey!

00:01:14.874 --> 00:01:15.942
It protects me!

00:01:15.942 --> 00:01:19.279
I don't have to worry about
X-rays or nuclear radiation.

00:01:19.279 --> 00:01:21.581
How can it hurt those humans?

00:01:21.581 --> 00:01:22.615
Nick Fiery: [with
outrage] I'm Sorry.

00:01:22.615 --> 00:01:26.085
I didn't create the
laws of the universe.

00:01:26.085 --> 00:01:27.954
Nick Fiery: You need to work
with Professor Plumbum here to

00:01:27.954 --> 00:01:31.057
recreate the newer, safer
version of yourself.

00:01:31.057 --> 00:01:32.992
Further instructions
are on your ship.

00:01:32.992 --> 00:01:35.195
Leadman: [with defiance] Hey,
I'll show this Professor Plumbum

00:01:35.195 --> 00:01:37.997
that I am fine
just the way I am.

00:01:37.997 --> 00:01:39.632
Leadman ain't
going to change.

00:01:39.632 --> 00:01:40.900
No sir.

00:01:46.139 --> 00:01:49.476
Envelope: This message will
self-destruct in 10 seconds.

00:01:49.476 --> 00:01:51.411
LM: I don't want to go
to some museum to

00:01:51.411 --> 00:01:53.346
find a flower painting.

00:01:53.346 --> 00:01:54.414
Are you kidding me?

00:01:54.414 --> 00:01:56.115
I'm a superhero!

00:01:56.115 --> 00:01:57.217
Flowers?

00:01:57.217 --> 00:01:58.151
Ugh.	

00:01:58.151 --> 00:02:00.453
[self-destruct noise]

00:02:00.453 --> 00:02:03.189
Leadman: Hah! - I wasn't
even singed by that.

00:02:03.189 --> 00:02:04.824
Go lead powers!

00:02:15.702 --> 00:02:18.972
LM: [obnoxiously] Those
flowers sure are pretty.

00:02:18.972 --> 00:02:21.307
PP: Yes, there are 82 of them.

00:02:21.307 --> 00:02:22.675
All are deadly.

00:02:22.675 --> 00:02:24.244
LM: They're flowers!

00:02:24.244 --> 00:02:26.145
PP: They are made of lead.

00:02:26.145 --> 00:02:30.450
LM: [Yawns] Oh yeah-I hear
there's a "lead problem"?

00:02:30.450 --> 00:02:32.285
I'm Leadman.

00:02:32.285 --> 00:02:34.120
PP: I'm Professor
Plumbum...

00:02:34.120 --> 00:02:35.922
LM: [with suspicion]
Professor Plumbum?

00:02:35.922 --> 00:02:37.357
In the library?

00:02:37.357 --> 00:02:38.925
With a lead pipe?!

00:02:38.925 --> 00:02:40.760
PP: [annoyed] That
was Professor Plum,

00:02:40.760 --> 00:02:43.162
I am Professor Plumbum.

00:02:43.162 --> 00:02:45.932
Though lead pipes are
involved in lead poisoning.

00:02:45.932 --> 00:02:46.533
LM: Uh...

00:02:46.533 --> 00:02:48.001
I'm confused.

00:02:48.001 --> 00:02:49.068
Poisoning?

00:02:49.068 --> 00:02:49.869
Lead?

00:02:49.869 --> 00:02:51.504
No, No, No...

00:02:51.504 --> 00:02:53.940
I'm the medicine
you need, baby.

00:02:53.940 --> 00:02:56.543
PP: [With sarcasm] That's not
surprising that you're confused.

00:02:56.543 --> 00:02:58.444
Look Leadman,
this is serious.

00:02:58.444 --> 00:03:00.413
For both of us.

00:03:00.413 --> 00:03:02.115
We have to recreate you.

00:03:02.115 --> 00:03:04.083
You cannot continue to
contact the humans in

00:03:04.083 --> 00:03:05.785
your current condition.

00:03:05.785 --> 00:03:06.986
LM: [excited] What do you mean?

00:03:06.986 --> 00:03:08.588
Look at this suit.

00:03:08.588 --> 00:03:10.657
Isn't it amazing??

00:03:10.657 --> 00:03:13.026
You can't mess with perfection.

00:03:13.026 --> 00:03:15.929
PP: [Sigh] Let's start
at the beginning.

00:03:15.929 --> 00:03:18.364
[time travel sound]

00:03:18.364 --> 00:03:20.900
Uh, okay, where are we now?

00:03:20.900 --> 00:03:23.403
PP: We are on the
Periodic Table of Elements.

00:03:23.403 --> 00:03:26.439
LM: I thought I was supposed
to have the superpowers.

00:03:26.439 --> 00:03:29.042
PP: [Ignores LM] Lead
is element number 82,

00:03:29.042 --> 00:03:31.277
with the symbol Pb.

00:03:32.812 --> 00:03:35.415
PP: It's a soft silver bluish
metal that quickly turns to

00:03:35.415 --> 00:03:37.517
a black color when
exposed to air.

00:03:37.517 --> 00:03:40.420
PP: Lead is a very useful
metal because-

00:03:40.420 --> 00:03:41.287
LM: [Interrupting, with
excitement] Number one!

00:03:41.287 --> 00:03:43.756
It has a low
melting point.

00:03:43.756 --> 00:03:45.491
PP: Melting point is the
temperature at which

00:03:45.491 --> 00:03:48.127
a solid melts to a liquid.

00:03:48.127 --> 00:03:48.995
LM: Lead can be melted into

00:03:48.995 --> 00:03:52.966
a liquid at much lower
temperatures than other metals.

00:03:53.900 --> 00:03:55.868
LM: And then made
into the desired shape.

00:03:55.868 --> 00:03:58.037
So I better knock it
off with the fire suit.

00:03:58.037 --> 00:03:58.805
LM: [with enthusiasm]
Number two!

00:03:58.805 --> 00:04:00.206
It is malleable -

00:04:00.206 --> 00:04:02.909
PP: That is, easy to form
into different shapes.

00:04:02.909 --> 00:04:08.181
LM: [grunts] I can be
flat like aluminum foil.

00:04:08.181 --> 00:04:12.785
LM: [grunts] I can be long
and thin like a pipe.

00:04:12.785 --> 00:04:16.155
LM: [grunts] Or I can
be round like a ball.

00:04:16.155 --> 00:04:18.758
Any shape really.

00:04:18.758 --> 00:04:19.759
LM: [With enthusiasm]
Number three!

00:04:19.759 --> 00:04:23.763
Lead is also very stable
over time and resists corrosion-

00:04:23.763 --> 00:04:25.231
PP: [interrupting] The
gradual wearing away by

00:04:25.231 --> 00:04:29.202
reacting with other
chemicals it's exposed to.

00:04:29.202 --> 00:04:33.239
[Sound of chemicals pouring
on Leadman, who is un-phased]

00:04:37.210 --> 00:04:39.846
LM: [Action-hero music]
I am Leadman!

00:04:39.846 --> 00:04:41.648
Lead is great!

00:04:41.648 --> 00:04:43.950
PP: These properties have
made lead very useful to

00:04:43.950 --> 00:04:45.685
mankind throughout history.

00:04:45.685 --> 00:04:47.987
PP: To its detriment,
as we'll see in a bit.

00:04:47.987 --> 00:04:49.355
[Vanishing sound:
LM and PP vanish]

00:04:51.624 --> 00:04:54.360
PP: Elemental lead-lead
just by itself-is

00:04:54.360 --> 00:04:56.829
extremely rare in nature.

00:04:56.829 --> 00:05:00.066
Lead compounds-mixtures of
lead and other elements-are

00:05:00.066 --> 00:05:03.403
found in the Earth's crust-the
part that we can walk on-and

00:05:03.403 --> 00:05:06.572
are still rare.

00:05:06.572 --> 00:05:09.042
LM: [with excitement] Oh
yeah, I'm a rare bird.

00:05:09.042 --> 00:05:13.613
So, why is it a problem to
humans if it's so rare, like me?

00:05:13.613 --> 00:05:16.282
PP: Lead is one of the
easiest metals to separate from

00:05:16.282 --> 00:05:19.619
other metals in compounds
mined out of the Earth's crust.

00:05:19.619 --> 00:05:22.422
Archeological research
has identified lead mines

00:05:22.422 --> 00:05:25.124
dating back to
6000 years ago.

00:05:25.124 --> 00:05:27.293
PP: Even before these
mines, lead compounds were

00:05:27.293 --> 00:05:28.995
used by ancient peoples.

00:05:28.995 --> 00:05:32.598
[Vanishing sound:
LM and PP vanish]

00:05:32.598 --> 00:05:35.334
PP: Lead sulfate-a black
compound-was used as

00:05:35.334 --> 00:05:37.403
eyeliner in the Stone Age.

00:05:37.403 --> 00:05:40.406
PP: Lead oxide-yellow and
red in color-was used to

00:05:40.406 --> 00:05:43.076
glaze pottery in 5000 B.C.

00:05:43.076 --> 00:05:45.545
PP: And pure lead figurines
have been excavated from

00:05:45.545 --> 00:05:49.348
Egyptian ruins dating
to 4000 B.C., along with

00:05:49.348 --> 00:05:52.251
weights, vessels,
and other metal work.

00:05:52.251 --> 00:05:56.089
PP: Lead coins have been
found dating to 3500 B.C. from

00:05:56.089 --> 00:05:58.224
both Assyria and China.

00:05:58.224 --> 00:06:00.660
[Vanishing sound:
LM and PP vanish]

00:06:00.660 --> 00:06:03.296
PP: Romans took lead
use to new depths.

00:06:04.731 --> 00:06:07.467
PP: With the manpower to
extract pure lead from mines-

00:06:07.467 --> 00:06:11.204
and great engineering-the Romans
used lead pipes in their

00:06:11.204 --> 00:06:15.208
aqueducts-water bridges-to
deliver waters to the cities.

00:06:15.208 --> 00:06:16.776
PP: Remember lead's
chemical symbol?

00:06:16.776 --> 00:06:18.010
It's Pb.

00:06:18.010 --> 00:06:21.714
The Latin word for lead was
plumbum, and the modern word

00:06:21.714 --> 00:06:24.250
plumber comes from this word.

00:06:24.250 --> 00:06:26.753
LM: [with confidence] Well,
that really means you're...

00:06:26.753 --> 00:06:28.287
Lead Woman.

00:06:28.287 --> 00:06:30.923
Don't fill yourself
with hate for lead.

00:06:30.923 --> 00:06:33.526
You need to be more
[brief pause] malleable.

00:06:33.526 --> 00:06:36.429
PP: Lead is sometimes
called the Roman metal.

00:06:36.429 --> 00:06:38.965
They used lead in all
facets of their lives,

00:06:38.965 --> 00:06:41.701
PP: From curse tablets,
sheets in Bath to lead

00:06:41.701 --> 00:06:44.771
compounds in their make-up
and paint products as well

00:06:44.771 --> 00:06:45.438
LM: [with enthusiasm] See!

00:06:45.438 --> 00:06:48.307
The Roman Empire glorified
lead-and nothing bad

00:06:48.307 --> 00:06:49.776
ever happened to them!

00:06:49.776 --> 00:06:52.044
You can't have
too much lead.

00:06:52.044 --> 00:06:53.379
PP: [ignoring LM] In
addition to the pipes,

00:06:53.379 --> 00:06:56.816
most households had pots and
drinking vessels made from lead.

00:06:56.816 --> 00:06:57.884
[Teleportation sounds]

00:06:57.884 --> 00:07:00.419
PP: Lead and its compounds
have a sweet taste.

00:07:00.419 --> 00:07:03.422
The Romans used lead
acetate as a food seasoning

00:07:03.422 --> 00:07:05.258
PP: and as a wine preservative.

00:07:06.425 --> 00:07:09.095
PP: They also concentrated
grape juice into syrup by

00:07:09.095 --> 00:07:10.963
boiling it in lead pots.

00:07:10.963 --> 00:07:13.132
The acid from the juice
would have caused lead to

00:07:13.132 --> 00:07:14.534
leech into the syrup.

00:07:14.534 --> 00:07:16.269
LM: [with enthusiasm] So
the humans liked lead,

00:07:16.269 --> 00:07:18.638
it was easy to work with,
they used it everywhere.

00:07:18.638 --> 00:07:20.206
How cool is that!

00:07:20.206 --> 00:07:23.476
PP: One small problem:
Lead and its compounds are

00:07:23.476 --> 00:07:26.779
toxic to humans-and
other animals-on Earth.

00:07:26.779 --> 00:07:29.081
LM: hmm-Toxic?

00:07:29.081 --> 00:07:30.850
What does that mean?

00:07:30.850 --> 00:07:34.587
PP: Toxic means poisonous-a
substance causing or capable of

00:07:34.587 --> 00:07:37.089
causing death or illness
if taken into the body.

00:07:38.457 --> 00:07:41.694
PP: Well, crying isn't going to
help your toxic little problem!

00:07:41.694 --> 00:07:43.496
LM: [defiantly] Fine!

00:07:43.496 --> 00:07:44.997
How does it get in?

00:07:44.997 --> 00:07:46.165
[Teleportation sounds]

00:07:46.165 --> 00:07:49.435
PP: There are 3 ways things
can be taken in, or absorbed,

00:07:49.435 --> 00:07:50.403
by the human body.

00:07:50.403 --> 00:07:53.472
PP: Number one: Through their
skin when they touch things.

00:07:53.472 --> 00:07:55.308
PP: Number two: Through
their stomachs and

00:07:55.308 --> 00:07:56.843
intestines when they eat.

00:07:56.843 --> 00:07:59.879
PP: and Number three: Through
their lungs when they breathe.

00:07:59.879 --> 00:08:02.548
LM: Well-I thought those
things protected the humans,

00:08:02.548 --> 00:08:05.384
like my lead suit protects me?

00:08:05.384 --> 00:08:07.220
PP: That's true for many things.

00:08:07.220 --> 00:08:10.456
But each of these is
made up of living cells.

00:08:10.456 --> 00:08:12.124
These cells absorb things from

00:08:12.124 --> 00:08:14.594
the environment
and the bloodstream.

00:08:14.594 --> 00:08:18.164
The lung and intestinal cells
will also absorb the lead.

00:08:18.164 --> 00:08:19.632
LM: What about the skin?

00:08:19.632 --> 00:08:21.200
Isn't that stuff dead?

00:08:21.200 --> 00:08:24.270
PP: The skin cells are truly
more protective cells, and

00:08:24.270 --> 00:08:27.740
in the case of lead,
do NOT absorb much lead.

00:08:27.740 --> 00:08:31.010
Just touching a lead object is
not going to poison the human.

00:08:31.010 --> 00:08:33.813
LM: What about eating
it-that sounds bad.

00:08:33.813 --> 00:08:37.049
PP: Once eaten, 20 to 50%
of the lead eaten will be

00:08:37.049 --> 00:08:39.552
absorbed into the
bloodstream of the human.

00:08:39.552 --> 00:08:43.356
Breathing it is worse - Of the
lead breathed in from the air,

00:08:43.356 --> 00:08:47.526
nearly 100% of the lead is
absorbed into the bloodstream.

00:08:47.526 --> 00:08:49.829
LM: [with curiosity] Whoa,
what does that mean?

00:08:49.829 --> 00:08:52.298
PP: Alright-if there were
10 grams of lead in the

00:08:52.298 --> 00:08:54.367
room with you and you ate it.

00:08:54.367 --> 00:08:58.471
You would absorb 2 to 5 grams of
the lead into your bloodstream.

00:08:58.471 --> 00:09:01.407
PP: If you breathed it
in-nearly all 10 grams of

00:09:01.407 --> 00:09:03.910
the lead would be absorbed
into your bloodstream.

00:09:03.910 --> 00:09:06.712
PP: The dose was the same
(10 grams), but the amount in

00:09:06.712 --> 00:09:09.615
the body is different
because of how it was taken in.

00:09:09.615 --> 00:09:11.217
PP: [with concern]
Another important point:

00:09:11.217 --> 00:09:14.020
Children absorb
MORE than adults.

00:09:14.020 --> 00:09:17.590
LM: So now it's in their
blood-how does it poison them?

00:09:17.590 --> 00:09:19.125
PP: Once in the
bloodstream, the lead can

00:09:19.125 --> 00:09:21.427
travel to all
parts of the body.

00:09:21.427 --> 00:09:23.829
It has different effects
depending upon which

00:09:23.829 --> 00:09:25.932
organ of the body
it is affecting.

00:09:25.932 --> 00:09:28.601
PP: The primary target
is the nervous system.

00:09:28.601 --> 00:09:30.870
PP: When lead is absorbed
by the cells of the brain,

00:09:30.870 --> 00:09:34.040
it causes permanent
damage, including: decreased

00:09:34.040 --> 00:09:38.377
intelligence [clears throat],
memory loss, paralysis, and

00:09:38.377 --> 00:09:41.981
decreased social skills [stares
at Leadman meaningfully].

00:09:41.981 --> 00:09:42.481
LM: Hey!

00:09:42.481 --> 00:09:44.483
We're talking about humans here.

00:09:44.483 --> 00:09:46.719
Lead doesn't affect my species.

00:09:46.719 --> 00:09:49.488
But why does it target
the human brain?

00:09:49.488 --> 00:09:51.390
PP: The brain is
sensitive to lead-

00:09:51.390 --> 00:09:54.093
a tiny amount
causes big problems.

00:09:54.093 --> 00:09:56.629
Other cells can handle the
lead a little bit better.

00:09:56.629 --> 00:09:59.365
LM: Give me the rest of
it-what else does it do?

00:09:59.365 --> 00:10:02.335
PP: In the guts-lead
poisoning causes cramping,

00:10:02.335 --> 00:10:04.103
pain, and constipation.

00:10:04.103 --> 00:10:06.339
PP: In the blood itself,
there are low levels of

00:10:06.339 --> 00:10:09.742
healthy red blood cells-the
ones that transport oxygen to

00:10:09.742 --> 00:10:11.410
all the cells of the body.

00:10:11.410 --> 00:10:15.481
Poisoned humans also show
reduced appetite and feel tired.

00:10:15.481 --> 00:10:17.883
PP: [sad voice] It damages
cells everywhere, in the

00:10:17.883 --> 00:10:21.554
lungs, the kidneys, and
the reproductive system.

00:10:21.554 --> 00:10:24.790
LM: [sad voice] How much
does it take to poison a human?

00:10:24.790 --> 00:10:27.526
PP: [sad voice] There is
no safe dosage of lead.

00:10:27.526 --> 00:10:31.163
A high dose all at once
results in acute lead poisoning,

00:10:31.163 --> 00:10:33.165
with symptoms affecting
the neurological and

00:10:33.165 --> 00:10:34.967
digestive systems the most.

00:10:34.967 --> 00:10:37.069
PP: [sad voice] Long-term
exposure to lower doses,

00:10:37.069 --> 00:10:39.438
results in chronic
lead poisoning.

00:10:39.438 --> 00:10:41.874
PP: [sad voice] The cells
are being damaged all over,

00:10:41.874 --> 00:10:44.910
but at a slower rate, so the
human doesn't appear to be

00:10:44.910 --> 00:10:47.813
poisoned because it
happens over a long time.

00:10:47.813 --> 00:10:50.583
PP: Even the Romans knew
about acute lead poisoning from

00:10:50.583 --> 00:10:53.619
observing their miners,
painters, and metal workers

00:10:53.619 --> 00:10:56.188
coming down with the
disease-didn't know about

00:10:56.188 --> 00:10:57.757
the chronic
poisoning, though.

00:10:57.757 --> 00:10:59.892
LM: So, the Romans
stopped using it.

00:10:59.892 --> 00:11:00.826
Right?

00:11:00.826 --> 00:11:02.595
Right?!?

00:11:02.595 --> 00:11:03.796
PP: [giggling] Do
you always follow

00:11:03.796 --> 00:11:06.065
the speed limit in
your spaceship??

00:11:06.065 --> 00:11:08.801
Knowing about the dangers
and not engaging in them

00:11:08.801 --> 00:11:10.569
are two different things.

00:11:10.569 --> 00:11:12.071
[Teleportation sounds]

00:11:12.071 --> 00:11:13.639
LM: [in the dark]
And where are we now?

00:11:13.639 --> 00:11:15.574
PP: The dark
ages of course.

00:11:16.909 --> 00:11:18.978
PP: Lead mining and use
were much lower during

00:11:18.978 --> 00:11:22.581
this time period up
until the 19th century.

00:11:22.581 --> 00:11:25.251
One notable exception was
the mechanical moveable

00:11:25.251 --> 00:11:26.786
type printing press.

00:11:26.786 --> 00:11:29.789
PP: Gutenberg combined several
existing technologies to create

00:11:29.789 --> 00:11:33.025
this press that used
lead-based printing blocks.

00:11:33.025 --> 00:11:35.961
PP: Lead was melted with tin
and antimony and poured into

00:11:35.961 --> 00:11:38.264
molds to form the
printing blocks.

00:11:38.264 --> 00:11:41.634
PP: Lead was also used to make
bullets and weights for fishing;

00:11:41.634 --> 00:11:44.270
remember, lead can be
melted over a regular

00:11:44.270 --> 00:11:47.573
wood fire, then melded
into the desired shape.

00:11:47.573 --> 00:11:49.742
LM: Those workers
don't look so good.

00:11:49.742 --> 00:11:52.645
LM: I thought lead wasn't
absorbed well through the skin?

00:11:52.645 --> 00:11:55.281
PP: That' true, the danger
comes from breathing the dust

00:11:55.281 --> 00:11:58.751
coming off the items as they
are handled or heated, and

00:11:58.751 --> 00:12:01.387
eating small amounts
from your hands and lips if

00:12:01.387 --> 00:12:04.223
they are not washed off
after working with the metal.

00:12:04.223 --> 00:12:07.326
LM: So humans are sickened by
me because of small amounts of

00:12:07.326 --> 00:12:09.895
lead dusts coming off of me?

00:12:09.895 --> 00:12:11.630
That can't be true!

00:12:11.630 --> 00:12:14.533
I use a dandruff
reducing shampoo!

00:12:14.533 --> 00:12:16.969
PP: Yes-the lead dust
coming off of you is

00:12:16.969 --> 00:12:20.005
a possibility for the
sickness that could happen.

00:12:20.005 --> 00:12:22.041
That's why we have
to recreate you.

00:12:22.041 --> 00:12:23.642
[Teleportation sounds]

00:12:23.642 --> 00:12:25.111
LM: Where are we now?

00:12:25.111 --> 00:12:26.278
PP: Focus!

00:12:26.278 --> 00:12:28.314
We're in the 1800s.

00:12:28.314 --> 00:12:31.150
LM: Ah, so WHEN
are we now...

00:12:31.150 --> 00:12:33.119
thought I was the superhero.

00:12:33.119 --> 00:12:36.388
Hey-I want one of these
time buttons you have here.

00:12:36.388 --> 00:12:37.223
PP: [annoyed] Focus!

00:12:37.223 --> 00:12:38.491
Focus!

00:12:38.491 --> 00:12:40.926
The industrial revolution
brought with it mining at

00:12:40.926 --> 00:12:43.028
levels never seen before.

00:12:43.028 --> 00:12:45.264
PP: Lead was easily
accessed and cheap, with

00:12:45.264 --> 00:12:46.966
many industrial uses.

00:12:46.966 --> 00:12:49.201
PP: Just as in Roman
times, people used it for

00:12:49.201 --> 00:12:52.004
pipes or to connect
copper pipes together.

00:12:52.004 --> 00:12:55.674
PP: As in Roman times, usually
as long as the water is flowing,

00:12:55.674 --> 00:12:58.511
very little lead from the
pipes gets into the water.

00:12:58.511 --> 00:12:59.678
[Teleportation sounds]

00:12:59.678 --> 00:13:02.414
PP: One of the largest problems
of modern times came with

00:13:02.414 --> 00:13:06.018
the use of lead carbonate,
which looks like this: (PbCO3)

00:13:06.018 --> 00:13:08.621
[Music]

00:13:08.621 --> 00:13:10.689
PP: It was used to produce
bright white color in

00:13:10.689 --> 00:13:11.991
indoor house paints.

00:13:11.991 --> 00:13:13.559
PP: Just as artists before had

00:13:13.559 --> 00:13:15.961
prized lead carbonate
for the same quality.

00:13:15.961 --> 00:13:17.296
[Teleportation sounds]

00:13:17.296 --> 00:13:19.064
PP: Lead-based paint was
used to paint virtually

00:13:19.064 --> 00:13:22.701
all indoor spaces
beginning in the 1800s

00:13:22.701 --> 00:13:24.236
PP: until the 1950s

00:13:24.236 --> 00:13:26.238
PP: before completely
phasing out by about

00:13:26.238 --> 00:13:29.041
1977 in the United States.

00:13:29.041 --> 00:13:31.911
PP: This paint is
toxic when eaten.

00:13:31.911 --> 00:13:35.814
LM: [giggling]
Wait-who's eating paint?

00:13:35.814 --> 00:13:37.850
PP: Remember how the
Romans used lead acetate

00:13:37.850 --> 00:13:39.952
as seasoning on their food?

00:13:39.952 --> 00:13:41.954
Lead tastes sweet to humans.

00:13:41.954 --> 00:13:43.055
[Teleportation sound]

00:13:43.055 --> 00:13:45.457
PP: Young children may find
paint chips on the floor,

00:13:45.457 --> 00:13:48.093
eat them and then look for more.

00:13:48.093 --> 00:13:50.563
People of all ages may
possibly breathe in

00:13:50.563 --> 00:13:52.298
the paint dust as well.

00:13:52.298 --> 00:13:53.732
LM: [with concern]
wait, wait! -hold it!

00:13:53.732 --> 00:13:57.503
So-now the humans are surrounded
by lead in their houses?

00:13:57.503 --> 00:14:00.573
PP: In a way-but-it gets worse.

00:14:00.573 --> 00:14:04.143
LM: They're surrounded
by it, drink it, and eat it.

00:14:04.143 --> 00:14:06.478
How does it get worse?

00:14:06.478 --> 00:14:08.948
PP: Henry Ford changed the
world with the creation of

00:14:08.948 --> 00:14:12.351
the first moving assembly
line car, the Model-T.

00:14:12.351 --> 00:14:14.553
PP: This made cars affordable
to working families,

00:14:14.553 --> 00:14:16.288
not just the very rich.

00:14:16.288 --> 00:14:18.924
The other car companies
quickly moved to copy the

00:14:18.924 --> 00:14:22.094
assembly line and make money
selling cars to everyone.

00:14:22.094 --> 00:14:24.296
PP: Competitor's
developed fancier cars,

00:14:24.296 --> 00:14:26.865
but their engines had problems.

00:14:26.865 --> 00:14:29.168
PP: Engineers found adding
a lead compound, 

00:14:29.168 --> 00:14:32.238
Tetraethyl lead (TEL),
fixed the engine's problems.

00:14:32.238 --> 00:14:35.441
LM: Ok-but correct me if
I'm wrong-I don't think

00:14:35.441 --> 00:14:38.143
people on this
planet eat cars.

00:14:38.143 --> 00:14:41.013
PP: Tetraethyl lead is
also known as TEL-and these

00:14:41.013 --> 00:14:44.316
TEL additives are extremely
toxic to produce and

00:14:44.316 --> 00:14:46.318
car exhausts emitted
the lead product

00:14:46.318 --> 00:14:48.320
directly into the ATMOSPHERE.

00:14:48.320 --> 00:14:51.890
PP: Remember, the body absorbs
up to 100% of the lead that is

00:14:51.890 --> 00:14:55.394
breathed in, so inhaling a
low dose of lead will cause

00:14:55.394 --> 00:14:58.497
more problems than eating
the same dose of lead.

00:14:58.497 --> 00:15:00.399
PP: It then causes all
those health problems

00:15:00.399 --> 00:15:01.967
we talked about earlier.

00:15:01.967 --> 00:15:06.405
LM: Well-this is not looking
good for the humans at all.

00:15:06.405 --> 00:15:07.906
PP: Calm down.

00:15:07.906 --> 00:15:09.408
There is good news.

00:15:09.408 --> 00:15:12.444
The toxic effects were
recognized, and legislation

00:15:12.444 --> 00:15:15.481
was passed to phase out
TEL additives starting in

00:15:15.481 --> 00:15:19.518
1973 and ending in
1986 in the U.S.

00:15:19.518 --> 00:15:21.553
PP: All leaded gas
additives were banned in

00:15:21.553 --> 00:15:24.556
the US by the end of 1995.

00:15:24.556 --> 00:15:27.660
Most countries today ban
leaded gas for automobiles.

00:15:27.660 --> 00:15:30.396
PP: The levels of lead in the
human bloodstream dropped by

00:15:30.396 --> 00:15:36.568
78% from 1978 to 1991 after
leaded gas was phased out.

00:15:36.568 --> 00:15:39.338
LM: What about all those
"lead" pencils humans use?

00:15:39.338 --> 00:15:40.773
What's up with that?

00:15:40.773 --> 00:15:44.143
PP: [giggling] So-called lead
pencils actually use graphite,

00:15:44.143 --> 00:15:47.346
a form of carbon that
is non-toxic to humans.

00:15:47.346 --> 00:15:49.548
It has its name because
originally they thought

00:15:49.548 --> 00:15:51.417
graphite was a form of lead.

00:15:51.417 --> 00:15:52.384
LM: Oh.

00:15:52.384 --> 00:15:53.118
Bummer.

00:15:53.118 --> 00:15:56.455
I told this girl one time
that I made the pencil.

00:15:56.455 --> 00:15:58.090
I got to make a call...

00:15:58.090 --> 00:16:00.459
LM: So where does that
leave the humans today?

00:16:00.459 --> 00:16:02.528
PP: Lead paint may be
found in buildings built

00:16:02.528 --> 00:16:07.633
before 1978, but it is safe if
it is not peeling or powdery.

00:16:07.633 --> 00:16:10.602
PP: Similarly, some houses
contain lead pipes.

00:16:10.602 --> 00:16:13.005
PP: Leaded fuel is banned
in most countries,

00:16:13.005 --> 00:16:15.374
and there is reduced lead
pollution in the air.

00:16:15.374 --> 00:16:18.344
PP: Lead is still used for
many industrial processes and

00:16:18.344 --> 00:16:21.847
products, and lead mining and
other industries continue to

00:16:21.847 --> 00:16:24.750
expose workers and
communities to airborne lead.

00:16:24.750 --> 00:16:28.153
PP: The 2 main uses today are
lead-based car batteries, and

00:16:28.153 --> 00:16:30.356
X-ray and radiation protection.

00:16:30.356 --> 00:16:33.258
PP: Other uses include
bullets and other ammunition,

00:16:33.258 --> 00:16:36.195
use in glass and
ceramics, metal molds,

00:16:36.195 --> 00:16:39.932
sheet lead (used in buildings,
roofs, to surround cables)

00:16:39.932 --> 00:16:41.734
PP: and sometimes
it gets included in

00:16:41.734 --> 00:16:45.704
jewelry, paint on
toys, cars, houses, etc.,

00:16:45.704 --> 00:16:49.475
homeopathic medicine,
fishing equipment, candy

00:16:49.475 --> 00:16:50.909
(remember the sweet taste?),

00:16:50.909 --> 00:16:52.945
and metal decorations.

00:16:52.945 --> 00:16:54.413
PP: So what did
we learn today?

00:16:54.413 --> 00:16:56.982
LM: The very qualities
that make lead so useful to

00:16:56.982 --> 00:17:00.586
humans also make
it extremely deadly.

00:17:00.586 --> 00:17:01.854
PP [surprised]: Exactly.

00:17:01.854 --> 00:17:04.423
Acute lead poisoning
by mining, painting,

00:17:04.423 --> 00:17:06.658
plumbing, and using
the printing press,

00:17:06.658 --> 00:17:08.260
was known in ancient times.

00:17:08.260 --> 00:17:10.529
PP: The effects of chronic
lead poisoning by the use of

00:17:10.529 --> 00:17:13.332
lead pipes, vessels,
paint, and other lead

00:17:13.332 --> 00:17:17.803
compounds over a lifetime was
only described in recent times.

00:17:17.803 --> 00:17:21.140
LM: Researchers continue to
study the levels of lead and to

00:17:21.140 --> 00:17:25.077
support legislation to protect humans and the planet Earth.

00:17:25.077 --> 00:17:27.579
PP: With some simple
precautions, humans can limit

00:17:27.579 --> 00:17:30.349
their exposure to lead
in their daily lives.

00:17:30.349 --> 00:17:31.517
LM: [excited] So, humans!

00:17:31.517 --> 00:17:33.752
Protect yourselves from lead!

00:17:33.752 --> 00:17:38.424
LM: #1-Don't play by old paint- you could eat it or inhale it.

00:17:38.424 --> 00:17:41.059
LM: Don't let young children
touch or play with it either.

00:17:41.059 --> 00:17:43.429
Also-don't play in
the dirt that may be

00:17:43.429 --> 00:17:45.431
contaminated with old paint.

00:17:45.431 --> 00:17:46.598
LM: And it's gross.

00:17:46.598 --> 00:17:47.866
LM: It's GROSS.

00:17:47.866 --> 00:17:48.934
Stop it.

00:17:48.934 --> 00:17:53.238
PP: #2-Flush old pipes with
COLD water for 30 seconds if

00:17:53.238 --> 00:17:55.941
the water is being used
for drinking or eating.

00:17:55.941 --> 00:17:58.944
This allows any lead that may
have come off the pipes into

00:17:58.944 --> 00:18:02.681
the water to flow out of
the pipe and into the drain.

00:18:02.681 --> 00:18:04.850
Water that is flowing
through the pipes-not

00:18:04.850 --> 00:18:07.653
sitting in them-is
very unlikely to be

00:18:07.653 --> 00:18:09.087
contaminated with lead.

00:18:09.087 --> 00:18:13.158
LM: #3-Use caution when buying
kid jewelry or toys or even

00:18:13.158 --> 00:18:15.694
candy manufactured in
countries that do not have

00:18:15.694 --> 00:18:18.063
strict laws on
the use of lead.

00:18:18.063 --> 00:18:20.599
Look for lead-free
or non-toxic labels.

00:18:20.599 --> 00:18:22.367
Different countries
have different laws

00:18:22.367 --> 00:18:24.002
on lead and its use.

00:18:24.002 --> 00:18:27.172
PP: #4-Thoroughly wash
your hands after handling

00:18:27.172 --> 00:18:30.809
any lead solids-after
science class, hunting,

00:18:30.809 --> 00:18:33.779
fishing, painting,
or handling pipes.

00:18:33.779 --> 00:18:36.448
LM: #5-Recycle car batteries!

00:18:36.448 --> 00:18:39.785
Limit the amount of lead that
gets into your local ecosystem.

00:18:39.785 --> 00:18:43.889
LM: And #6-[embarrassed] Don't
go to my apartment right now.

00:18:43.889 --> 00:18:46.125
I-I have a lot of cleaning
to do apparently.

00:18:46.125 --> 00:18:47.259
I've learned a lot today.

00:18:47.259 --> 00:18:48.961
D-Don't-don't-don't come by.

00:18:48.961 --> 00:18:53.098
We're going to move the
party to next weekend-ladies.

00:18:53.098 --> 00:18:55.000
PP: [giggling]
Anything else?

00:18:55.000 --> 00:18:59.104
LM: Yeah-humans-uh wash your
hands before eating or drinking!

00:18:59.104 --> 00:19:01.206
Leave your shoes
at the front door.

00:19:01.206 --> 00:19:03.842
Um-don't wear a lead
suit all the time-

00:19:03.842 --> 00:19:07.112
LM: It's okay for your dentist
to put one on for X-rays-the

00:19:07.112 --> 00:19:09.281
lead in those aprons is
completely contained and

00:19:09.281 --> 00:19:13.485
can't leak out.

00:19:13.485 --> 00:19:17.289
PP: Knowledge is power-in
this case, protective power.

00:19:17.289 --> 00:19:18.257
PP: Leadman?

00:19:18.257 --> 00:19:20.425
PP: [annoyed]-Leadman?!?

00:19:20.425 --> 00:19:21.927
LM: Yeah-no-uh-That's

00:19:21.927 --> 00:19:25.330
Uranium-Plutonium
Fusion Man now.

00:19:25.330 --> 00:19:26.331
LM: Thank you.

00:19:39.511 --> 00:19:42.047
NF: This has been a presentation of the U.S. National Library of

00:19:42.047 --> 00:19:45.117
Medicine Division of Specialized
Information Services.

00:19:45.117 --> 00:19:47.019
Visit the Environmental
Health Student Portal for

00:19:47.019 --> 00:19:48.654
more information on lead.

00:19:48.654 --> 00:19:49.988
NF: [with authority]
Dismissed, soldier!

